WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.638
SD-WAN edges can intercept DNS no matter
which resolver a client was given.

2
00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:09.688
In the book's Cisco example,
that's the redirect-dns action.

3
00:00:09.688 --> 00:00:12.417
The point of this slide
is that the destination

4
00:00:12.417 --> 00:00:15.076
decides whether agency
policy applies at all.

5
00:00:15.076 --> 00:00:18.558
Sent to an agency resolver,
the query gets block lists,

6
00:00:18.558 --> 00:00:23.546
validation and logging, and internet
lookups leave through Protective DNS.

7
00:00:23.546 --> 00:00:27.900
Sent to an outside resolver, it gets
none of that, and nothing alerts.

8
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.962
The feature has limits too; it handles
only IPv4 over UDP, for instance.

9
00:00:33.962 --> 00:00:38.289
Two refinements: the target must
be a server the edge can address,

10
00:00:38.289 --> 00:00:40.412
not a loopback agent on a laptop.

11
00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:45.842
And even a FedRAMP-listed SASE resolver
bypasses the agency's own plane, so the

12
00:00:45.842 --> 00:00:51.500
redirect target is always the agency
resolver, with any SASE resolver behind it.
